WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.976 --> 00:00:05.796
All right.

00:00:05.796 --> 00:00:09.126
So let me start by
motivating IPyShiny

00:00:09.126 --> 00:00:12.285
with this interactive
web application.

00:00:12.285 --> 00:00:16.656
So in Core Shiny itself,
we have the ability

00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:21.066
to only render like a
few different things

00:00:21.066 --> 00:00:23.706
like images, and
plots, and text.

00:00:23.706 --> 00:00:27.306
Like some very
basic core utilities

00:00:27.306 --> 00:00:29.766
for rendering Python objects.

00:00:29.766 --> 00:00:33.196
Let me mention this is also
very experimental at this point.

00:00:33.196 --> 00:00:35.526
And partially the
reason why it's

00:00:35.526 --> 00:00:37.476
in a separate
package at this point

00:00:37.476 --> 00:00:41.736
is we've been experimenting
with the ability

00:00:41.736 --> 00:00:45.876
to render IPywidgets with Shiny.

00:00:45.876 --> 00:00:49.536
So if you're not already
familiar with IPywidgets

00:00:49.536 --> 00:00:54.846
it provides this foundation
for Python developers

00:00:54.846 --> 00:01:02.376
to create a Python package that
wraps a JavaScript library.

00:01:02.376 --> 00:01:07.596
A library like leaflet for
creating interactive maps.

00:01:07.596 --> 00:01:10.086
And provide an
interface in Python

00:01:10.086 --> 00:01:16.746
that people can then use
that Python package to create

00:01:16.746 --> 00:01:20.556
these interactive
data visualizations

00:01:20.556 --> 00:01:21.726
inside of Jupyter.

00:01:21.726 --> 00:01:27.366
So traditionally, the main
platform for using IPywidget's

00:01:27.366 --> 00:01:28.416
Jupyter.

00:01:28.416 --> 00:01:34.326
But we've provided
this wrapper to allow

00:01:34.326 --> 00:01:37.656
you to write the
same IPywidgets code

00:01:37.656 --> 00:01:45.096
and just get that into
Shiny in the way that

00:01:45.096 --> 00:01:46.336
makes the most sense to you.

00:01:46.336 --> 00:01:52.206
So this is a motivating example
where I have some data here.

00:01:52.206 --> 00:01:56.406
This is showing
a superZIP score.

00:01:56.406 --> 00:01:59.436
And these superZIP
scores are really

00:01:59.436 --> 00:02:05.616
like some combination of
percent college educated,

00:02:05.616 --> 00:02:11.436
median income, and
I think population

00:02:11.436 --> 00:02:14.916
for each ZIP code in the US.

00:02:14.916 --> 00:02:17.796
So you can go to this
dropdown, and instead

00:02:17.796 --> 00:02:22.266
of looking at some combination
of income and education,

00:02:22.266 --> 00:02:24.946
essentially, you can
look at just education.

00:02:24.946 --> 00:02:30.516
So you can see compared
to this overall score,

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:34.296
percent college educated
is a lot more highly densed

00:02:34.296 --> 00:02:38.046
in the Northeast in the US.

00:02:38.046 --> 00:02:40.266
And then you can see
median income is actually

00:02:40.266 --> 00:02:46.026
maybe more highly correlated
with this overall score.

00:02:46.026 --> 00:02:48.096
It's at least more
wide spread out.

00:02:48.096 --> 00:02:51.456
Basically, three
different main measures

00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:53.436
that we're looking at here.

00:02:53.436 --> 00:02:59.646
And then this is a density
plot of the overall score

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:02.596
for all of the US essentially.

00:03:02.596 --> 00:03:04.986
So you can see
this overall score,

00:03:04.986 --> 00:03:10.216
there's a pretty high
concentration around 0 to 20.

00:03:10.216 --> 00:03:16.296
And same similar for college
but it's more pronounced.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:19.926
And pretty similar
distribution for income.

00:03:19.926 --> 00:03:25.536
And then this is the
log of the population.

00:03:25.536 --> 00:03:28.186
If I didn't take the
log here it'd be super--

00:03:28.186 --> 00:03:31.896
you'd basically just see
a straight line up here

00:03:31.896 --> 00:03:35.286
and then a very long tail.

00:03:35.286 --> 00:03:38.916
And the point of having these
density plots here is this

00:03:38.916 --> 00:03:41.376
is programmed in such a
way that I can zoom in

00:03:41.376 --> 00:03:43.296
on this application.

00:03:43.296 --> 00:03:46.116
And these density
plots will update.

00:03:46.116 --> 00:03:48.996
Whenever I change the
bounding box of this map,

00:03:48.996 --> 00:03:51.966
I will recompute the
density of these things

00:03:51.966 --> 00:03:57.346
conditional on just the
ZIP codes within this area.

00:03:57.346 --> 00:04:02.346
So as I zoom in
here to the DC area,

00:04:02.346 --> 00:04:06.366
you can see the in
bound's density here,

00:04:06.366 --> 00:04:11.946
there's a much higher density
of very high overall scores

00:04:11.946 --> 00:04:14.585
in the DC area.

00:04:14.585 --> 00:04:19.716
And that's really a combination
of the percent college

00:04:19.716 --> 00:04:22.626
educated and the
income both being

00:04:22.626 --> 00:04:25.086
much higher in these
areas, as well as

00:04:25.086 --> 00:04:27.066
a higher population I guess.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:29.056
Population density I should say.

00:04:29.056 --> 00:04:34.356
And then if you want to get more
detailed into what specifically

00:04:34.356 --> 00:04:39.006
is in this view, there's
this Details tab over here

00:04:39.006 --> 00:04:42.216
that tells me there's
123 ZIP codes currently

00:04:42.216 --> 00:04:45.246
in that bounding box.

00:04:45.246 --> 00:04:52.686
And amongst those 123 ZIPs,
23% are considered superZIPs.

00:04:52.686 --> 00:04:56.556
Which just means their score
is above a certain threshold

00:04:56.556 --> 00:05:00.186
and their mean income
is nearly $100,000.

00:05:00.186 --> 00:05:04.466
The mean population is
a little over 10,000,

00:05:04.466 --> 00:05:08.486
and the mean college
educated is nearly 40%.

00:05:08.486 --> 00:05:09.596
And then if I wanted--

00:05:09.596 --> 00:05:15.326
if I had other data
about these ZIP codes,

00:05:15.326 --> 00:05:18.896
I can see other information
here like their relative rank

00:05:18.896 --> 00:05:23.016
amongst all of the ZIP
codes and stuff like that.

00:05:23.016 --> 00:05:26.066
And this is also hooked
up to another map where

00:05:26.066 --> 00:05:29.366
if I want to click on
a particular County

00:05:29.366 --> 00:05:33.986
and see exactly where
that County is I

00:05:33.986 --> 00:05:40.826
can zoom in on this map
and I can see where this--

00:05:40.826 --> 00:05:41.326
sorry.

00:05:41.326 --> 00:05:44.186
ZIP code is actually located.

00:05:44.186 --> 00:05:45.466
And this is currently a bug.

00:05:45.466 --> 00:05:48.346
When I click on this pop
up the table goes away,

00:05:48.346 --> 00:05:51.646
but hopefully that will
get fixed sometime soon.

00:05:51.646 --> 00:05:55.966
Where I could click on
this marker as well to see,

00:05:55.966 --> 00:05:58.516
basically, that same
information displayed again.

00:05:58.516 --> 00:06:03.406
So the repo's name is ipshiny.

00:06:03.406 --> 00:06:06.616
If you go to the
GitHub page, it will

00:06:06.616 --> 00:06:09.346
have some information
on how to install it

00:06:09.346 --> 00:06:12.206
and a quick overview
on how to use it.

00:06:12.206 --> 00:06:13.846
There's a couple
of different ways

00:06:13.846 --> 00:06:19.936
you can actually render
an IPwidget with IPyShiny.

00:06:19.936 --> 00:06:24.016
And the first use
that it goes over

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:26.176
is-- it should feel
really intuitive

00:06:26.176 --> 00:06:33.106
if you are already an IPwidget
user instead of a Shiny user.

00:06:33.106 --> 00:06:34.876
So hopefully most
of the audiences

00:06:34.876 --> 00:06:41.746
in that former group where you
want to always within a Shiny

00:06:41.746 --> 00:06:44.326
app create your user interface.

00:06:44.326 --> 00:06:49.336
And in that user interface,
use this output widget

00:06:49.336 --> 00:06:52.396
which comes from IPyShiny.

00:06:52.396 --> 00:06:57.466
And like any other
UI component, you

00:06:57.466 --> 00:06:59.416
want to give it an
ID that you'll then

00:06:59.416 --> 00:07:02.536
reference on the server.

00:07:02.536 --> 00:07:05.176
And inside of the
server function,

00:07:05.176 --> 00:07:07.666
the recommended
way to do this is

00:07:07.666 --> 00:07:16.246
to first initialize the widget
object when the server first--

00:07:16.246 --> 00:07:18.986
server function first executes.

00:07:18.986 --> 00:07:21.106
So this will only run once.

00:07:21.106 --> 00:07:26.806
We'll be initializing the
map just once with a center

00:07:26.806 --> 00:07:30.106
location and a zoom level.

00:07:30.106 --> 00:07:32.446
And then once you're
ready, you want

00:07:32.446 --> 00:07:36.526
to call register
widget with the ID that

00:07:36.526 --> 00:07:40.966
should match this ID on the
user interface to basically say,

00:07:40.966 --> 00:07:44.566
I have my widget object here
that I want you to perform

00:07:44.566 --> 00:07:47.836
like an initial draw of--

00:07:47.836 --> 00:07:49.066
for the application.

00:07:49.066 --> 00:07:53.566
And then for all of
the other updates,

00:07:53.566 --> 00:07:55.666
you're actually
performing side effects.

00:07:55.666 --> 00:07:57.706
You're not like redrawing--

00:07:57.706 --> 00:07:59.776
recreating the
widget from scratch,

00:07:59.776 --> 00:08:04.096
you're essentially just mutating
properties on that widget.

00:08:04.096 --> 00:08:07.006
So it's a little bit different
if you're coming from a Shiny

00:08:07.006 --> 00:08:12.076
world, where we
essentially reinitialize

00:08:12.076 --> 00:08:14.446
and redraw a widget
every time we want

00:08:14.446 --> 00:08:18.086
to update it using our code.

00:08:18.086 --> 00:08:23.336
But here, we will register,
essentially, side effects.

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:25.636
So we haven't seen
this yet from Shiny,

00:08:25.636 --> 00:08:30.855
but this is a decorator that
comes from the Shiny reactive

00:08:30.855 --> 00:08:32.306
sub package.

00:08:32.306 --> 00:08:34.786
So we saw reactive calc before.

00:08:34.786 --> 00:08:38.716
Where this is-- a calc,
you might remember,

00:08:38.716 --> 00:08:40.676
wants you to return a value.

00:08:40.676 --> 00:08:45.706
So it reads some other reactive
values and then returns

00:08:45.706 --> 00:08:48.046
a reactive value.

00:08:48.046 --> 00:08:53.806
But with side effects, this
also can read reactive values,

00:08:53.806 --> 00:08:55.306
but you're not
going to be calling

00:08:55.306 --> 00:08:58.076
this function for
a return value,

00:08:58.076 --> 00:09:02.486
you're going to be calling this
function for its side effects.

00:09:02.486 --> 00:09:06.826
So when I say reactive
effect, I don't really

00:09:06.826 --> 00:09:09.556
need to give this a
name because Shiny isn't

00:09:09.556 --> 00:09:11.486
going to put this anywhere.

00:09:11.486 --> 00:09:14.176
So I can just use this
underscore and define

00:09:14.176 --> 00:09:15.776
a function.

00:09:15.776 --> 00:09:21.026
And then I will read
the zoom input value,

00:09:21.026 --> 00:09:24.556
which is coming from
our Shiny input slider

00:09:24.556 --> 00:09:26.776
up here at the zoom.

00:09:26.776 --> 00:09:31.906
And that is going to then--

00:09:31.906 --> 00:09:33.526
what this is
essentially saying is

00:09:33.526 --> 00:09:36.376
whenever the slider
changes, update

00:09:36.376 --> 00:09:39.376
the map's zoom attribute.

00:09:39.376 --> 00:09:44.476
So map.zoom is like
an IPywidget property

00:09:44.476 --> 00:09:49.486
that will be mutating every time
the value of this zoom input

00:09:49.486 --> 00:09:50.486
value changes.

00:09:50.486 --> 00:09:50.986
All right.

00:09:50.986 --> 00:09:53.146
So we just went
over the side effect

00:09:53.146 --> 00:09:57.886
that allows me to change
this input slider,

00:09:57.886 --> 00:10:02.656
to change the zoom
level on the map.

00:10:02.656 --> 00:10:07.156
But what if I wanted to go the
other way in the sense of I

00:10:07.156 --> 00:10:13.726
can also use this control that's
directly attached to the widget

00:10:13.726 --> 00:10:15.976
instance on the IPywidget?

00:10:15.976 --> 00:10:17.896
If I wanted to zoom in this way.

00:10:17.896 --> 00:10:19.906
What if I wanted
to, essentially,

00:10:19.906 --> 00:10:23.506
have that bidirectional
communication of changing

00:10:23.506 --> 00:10:28.856
the zoom on the map,
update the slider as well?

00:10:28.856 --> 00:10:31.876
So in order to do something
like this where you're

00:10:31.876 --> 00:10:36.616
kind of treating the IPywidget
like an input control,

00:10:36.616 --> 00:10:42.956
to then update some other
thing on your user interface.

00:10:42.956 --> 00:10:47.656
Then you want to use
this helper function,

00:10:47.656 --> 00:10:52.666
that again comes
from IPyShiny, to--

00:10:52.666 --> 00:10:55.576
this reactive read
essentially allows

00:10:55.576 --> 00:11:00.916
me to read this zoom property on
the map object in the same way

00:11:00.916 --> 00:11:04.396
that I would read like
in a true Shiny input

00:11:04.396 --> 00:11:06.406
value like this input.zoom.

00:11:06.406 --> 00:11:10.306
By using this reactive read here
inside of this reactive side

00:11:10.306 --> 00:11:12.706
effect, that will
ensure whenever

00:11:12.706 --> 00:11:15.466
the zoom property
changes on this map,

00:11:15.466 --> 00:11:19.366
this function is going
to re-execute and perform

00:11:19.366 --> 00:11:21.896
side effects.

00:11:21.896 --> 00:11:24.286
So I'll get the zoom
level from the map

00:11:24.286 --> 00:11:30.166
here, and use that to
inform this function that's

00:11:30.166 --> 00:11:33.536
coming from Shiny to
update the slider.

00:11:33.536 --> 00:11:36.076
So this is coming from
the UI sub package

00:11:36.076 --> 00:11:40.396
to say, update this
slider with an ID of zoom,

00:11:40.396 --> 00:11:44.966
and update its value to whatever
the zoom level is on the map.

00:11:44.966 --> 00:11:45.466
Right?

00:11:45.466 --> 00:11:47.086
So this is like--

00:11:47.086 --> 00:11:49.426
it changes from the
slider to the map.

00:11:49.426 --> 00:11:52.266
And if you want to go the other
way from the map to the slider,

00:11:52.266 --> 00:11:53.641
you could do
something like this.

00:11:56.636 --> 00:12:00.256
And then this last
part here is basically

00:12:00.256 --> 00:12:06.766
to get this text up here
showing the current latitude

00:12:06.766 --> 00:12:10.996
and longitude on the bounding
box of this map, which

00:12:10.996 --> 00:12:13.816
will change whenever
this zoom level changes.

00:12:13.816 --> 00:12:19.216
But I can also pan on the map
to get a different bounding box

00:12:19.216 --> 00:12:21.376
with different
latitude and longitude.

00:12:21.376 --> 00:12:23.846
If I wanted to do
something like this,

00:12:23.846 --> 00:12:28.696
this is available on the
maps bounds attribute.

00:12:28.696 --> 00:12:33.286
So normally, if you wanted to
read this property in a Jupyter

00:12:33.286 --> 00:12:39.166
context, you would just type
out map.bounds, and then

00:12:39.166 --> 00:12:41.266
enter on your console,
and that would give you

00:12:41.266 --> 00:12:43.336
the current bounds

00:12:43.336 --> 00:12:46.336
But we want to
re-actively read this.

00:12:46.336 --> 00:12:49.336
Whenever the bounds
change, we want

00:12:49.336 --> 00:12:55.616
this output to get invalidated
and basically, re-execute.

00:12:55.616 --> 00:12:59.296
So that's why we use
the reactive read here.

00:12:59.296 --> 00:13:00.916
And then from this
bounds I'm just

00:13:00.916 --> 00:13:08.296
getting the latitude and
longitude in a more usable way.

00:13:08.296 --> 00:13:11.086
And then returning a string
with the current latitude

00:13:11.086 --> 00:13:14.086
and longitude and
rendering that as text.

00:13:14.086 --> 00:13:18.226
Yeah, that basically
kind of covers

00:13:18.226 --> 00:13:20.956
the core of what you
would need to do something

00:13:20.956 --> 00:13:25.336
like coordinate other controls
with a widget in terms

00:13:25.336 --> 00:13:28.456
of keeping its state in
sync with other things

00:13:28.456 --> 00:13:29.776
in your application.

00:13:29.776 --> 00:13:33.586
And also keep in mind that if
you are coming from our Shiny

00:13:33.586 --> 00:13:39.436
world, and this doesn't make
a whole lot of sense to you,

00:13:39.436 --> 00:13:44.206
or it's a foreign programming
paradigm that you're not

00:13:44.206 --> 00:13:51.106
so keen on using, we
do also, in IPyShiny,

00:13:51.106 --> 00:13:57.046
provide a more render
text like interface.

00:13:57.046 --> 00:14:00.656
You can initialize widgets
inside of a reactive context.

00:14:00.656 --> 00:14:03.826
So there might be
situations where you really

00:14:03.826 --> 00:14:10.306
do want the sum parameter that
goes into initializing a widget

00:14:10.306 --> 00:14:13.366
to come from a reactive value.

00:14:13.366 --> 00:14:17.956
Before, we just had a
hard coded value of 4.

00:14:17.956 --> 00:14:20.896
But if you just
needed your slider

00:14:20.896 --> 00:14:23.896
to redraw the map for
instance every time

00:14:23.896 --> 00:14:26.836
you change the slider,
you could do something

00:14:26.836 --> 00:14:30.076
like this, which would
reinitialize and redraw

00:14:30.076 --> 00:14:33.256
from scratch that map object.

00:14:33.256 --> 00:14:40.246
Which if that redrawing
is relatively cheap,

00:14:40.246 --> 00:14:43.006
that can be a totally
fine way to approach how

00:14:43.006 --> 00:14:45.796
to render widgets with Shiny.

00:14:45.796 --> 00:14:47.926
And again, this is going
to feel like a little bit

00:14:47.926 --> 00:14:51.856
more natural in some
cases for Shiny users

00:14:51.856 --> 00:14:54.956
because you're not having
to deal with side effects.

00:14:54.956 --> 00:14:59.086
And we generally recommend
avoiding side effects

00:14:59.086 --> 00:15:02.176
as much as possible, because
they can make your application

00:15:02.176 --> 00:15:03.826
logic harder to reason about.

00:15:03.826 --> 00:15:07.126
Yeah this other option
is available to you,

00:15:07.126 --> 00:15:10.726
but to really highlight a
situation where you do really

00:15:10.726 --> 00:15:15.226
want the side effects is maybe
an example like this, where

00:15:15.226 --> 00:15:21.436
I'm using plotly to render
a scatterplot with say

00:15:21.436 --> 00:15:23.656
10,000 or maybe more points.

00:15:23.656 --> 00:15:25.916
Don't worry too much
about this code.

00:15:25.916 --> 00:15:30.106
This code is just fitting
a linear regression

00:15:30.106 --> 00:15:33.916
to 10,000 different
random x-coordinates

00:15:33.916 --> 00:15:36.326
and y-coordinates.

00:15:36.326 --> 00:15:43.096
And then I'm going to create
a scatterplot down here

00:15:43.096 --> 00:15:43.786
using plotly.

00:15:46.636 --> 00:15:53.206
But really the key part here is
I'm using this register widget

00:15:53.206 --> 00:16:00.406
way of basically doing all of
the work ahead of time to say,

00:16:00.406 --> 00:16:02.956
here's my scatterplot, I know
that this thing isn't going

00:16:02.956 --> 00:16:05.656
to need to be redrawn, so
I'm going to initialize

00:16:05.656 --> 00:16:10.096
with the scatterplot, register
it to do that initial draw,

00:16:10.096 --> 00:16:13.756
and then I will basically
register the side

00:16:13.756 --> 00:16:14.866
effect to happen.

00:16:14.866 --> 00:16:19.496
Whenever I click this
Show fit button here,

00:16:19.496 --> 00:16:22.906
which is tied to this
checkbox, then I just

00:16:22.906 --> 00:16:28.006
want to basically toggle the
visibility of this fitted line

00:16:28.006 --> 00:16:34.656
that I've added here inside
of the figure widget object.

00:16:34.656 --> 00:16:37.866
Right, so the scatterplot is
actually two different pieces.

00:16:37.866 --> 00:16:40.536
One the actual markers
is the black dots

00:16:40.536 --> 00:16:43.206
here for the
scatterplot, but then,

00:16:43.206 --> 00:16:47.886
I've also layered
on a fitted line

00:16:47.886 --> 00:16:51.466
that thankfully with like
the way plotly is set up,

00:16:51.466 --> 00:16:54.276
you can then mutate
like the visibility

00:16:54.276 --> 00:17:00.246
of that specific line traces,
what plotly would call this.

00:17:00.246 --> 00:17:05.346
So if you tried to do something
like this where instead imagine

00:17:05.346 --> 00:17:08.016
you were creating this
figure widget object

00:17:08.016 --> 00:17:15.486
inside of a render widget
context, every time you--

00:17:15.486 --> 00:17:18.425
every time the show
fit value changed

00:17:18.425 --> 00:17:21.665
you would have to
reinitialize and redraw

00:17:21.665 --> 00:17:23.836
a ton of different points.

00:17:23.836 --> 00:17:28.326
And you would get a bit
of lag because every time

00:17:28.326 --> 00:17:30.856
you click it has to
redo all of that work.

00:17:30.856 --> 00:17:33.996
So yeah, it's kind
of a line that people

00:17:33.996 --> 00:17:36.936
will have to walk in the
sense that like this is really

00:17:36.936 --> 00:17:40.866
easy and quick to get started
with something displaying

00:17:40.866 --> 00:17:42.606
inside of Shiny.

00:17:42.606 --> 00:17:45.696
But if you're in a
situation where you really

00:17:45.696 --> 00:17:48.246
need performant
updates, other outputs

00:17:48.246 --> 00:17:51.636
don't depend on
your widget state,

00:17:51.636 --> 00:17:53.886
and it's really
convenient to initialize.

00:17:53.886 --> 00:17:58.366
Like, this is a handy way to go.

00:17:58.366 --> 00:18:01.566
But in the case where
you need performance,

00:18:01.566 --> 00:18:03.826
this is going to
be really useful.

00:18:03.826 --> 00:18:05.376
So--

